A = {
"af15919": dict(
 bluf="Morocco's invasion across the Sahara destroyed Songhai, the last of the great Sahel empires, with a small army of musketeers. The Niger region broke into smaller states, and West Africa's commercial centre of gravity shifted from the desert routes toward the Atlantic coast, where Europeans traded.",
 why="Sultan Ahmad al-Mansur wanted the gold of the Sahel to fund his state and compete with the Ottomans. A disputed succession had weakened Songhai.",
 then="The Moroccan commanders in Timbuktu soon became independent of Marrakesh, and the region fragmented. Gold never flowed north in the amounts al-Mansur hoped, and leading Timbuktu scholars were deported to Morocco.",
 now="Timbuktu's decline after the conquest became the symbol of the end of West Africa's imperial age; no single state has controlled the Niger bend since.",
 lens="Firearms let small, disciplined forces beat large traditional armies, but conquering is easier than governing from across a desert."),
"as159214": dict(
 bluf="Hideyoshi's invasions of Korea (1592-98) were East Asia's largest war of the century, drawing in Ming China and devastating Korea. They failed, but they left a lasting Korean memory of Japanese aggression and drained the Ming at a critical moment.",
 why="Having unified Japan, Hideyoshi needed new conquests to reward his lords and armies, and he aimed to replace China at the centre of the East Asian order.",
 trig="Korea refused to let Japanese armies pass through on their way to attack China.",
 then="Korea was ravaged: cities burned, many thousands killed, and potters and artisans carried off to Japan. The cost of intervening helped empty the Ming treasury, a factor in the dynasty's later collapse. Japan withdrew after Hideyoshi's death, and the Tokugawa later restored relations.",
 now="Admiral Yi and the 'Imjin War' are central to Korean national memory and still colour attitudes toward Japan.",
 lens="Korea's geography makes it the bridge, and the battlefield, between continental and maritime powers."),
"eu159827": dict(
 bluf="The Edict of Nantes ended France's religious wars by granting Protestants limited toleration, an early case of a European state accepting two faiths in order to secure peace. Its revocation in 1685 showed how fragile tolerance was when it rested on a king's will.",
 why="Neither side could win, and Henry IV, a former Protestant, needed Huguenot loyalty as well as Catholic acceptance to rebuild royal authority.",
 then="The Huguenots kept fortified towns, which Cardinal Richelieu saw as a 'state within a state' and took away in 1628. Louis XIV revoked the edict in 1685; about 200,000 Huguenots fled to the Netherlands, England, Prussia and South Africa, taking their skills with them.",
 now="It is an early example of a state choosing tolerance to end religious war, and its revocation an early example of persecution causing a brain drain.",
 lens="Toleration granted by one ruler can be revoked by the next; durable rights need institutions that outlast the ruler."),
"me-abbas": dict(
 bluf="Shah Abbas rebuilt Safavid Iran into a strong centralized state: he created a standing army loyal to the crown, recovered lost territory and made Isfahan a showcase capital. His reforms let Iran hold its own between the Ottomans and Mughals for another century.",
 why="Abbas inherited an empire losing land to the Ottomans and Uzbeks and dominated by Qizilbash tribal chiefs. He needed forces loyal to him alone.",
 then="He built musketeer and artillery corps from converted Caucasian slave-soldiers, retook Tabriz and Baghdad, expelled the Portuguese from Hormuz with English help (1622), and promoted the silk trade. His habit of confining royal princes in the palace produced weak successors.",
 now="Isfahan's Naqsh-e Jahan square is a UNESCO site and a symbol of Iranian national culture.",
 lens="Rulers who build armies loyal only to themselves can break the power of hereditary elites, but such systems depend on capable rulers."),
"as160015": dict(
 bluf="Sekigahara decided who would inherit Hideyoshi's unified Japan. Tokugawa Ieyasu's victory led to 250 years of Tokugawa rule, and the line between his allies and his defeated enemies shaped Japanese politics until 1868.",
 why="Hideyoshi's death in 1598 left a child heir, and the regents he appointed fought for power.",
 then="Ieyasu became shogun in 1603, confiscated or cut the lands of the losers, and placed trusted allies in strategic domains. Defeated 'outside' lords, notably Chōshū and Satsuma, nursed their grievances for two and a half centuries and led the overthrow of the shogunate in 1868.",
 now="'Sekigahara' is still Japanese shorthand for a decisive showdown.",
 lens="The losers of a decisive civil war can shape politics for centuries: the defeated of 1600 became the revolutionaries of 1868."),
"eu160028": dict(
 bluf="A trading company chartered by the English crown became, over two centuries, the ruler of most of India. It is history's clearest case of a corporation turning into a state, with an army, tax powers and its own foreign policy, and it was the vehicle through which Britain built its Asian empire.",
 story="In 1600 Elizabeth I granted a group of London merchants a monopoly on English trade east of the Cape of Good Hope. Outcompeted by the Dutch in the spice islands, the Company concentrated on India, trading cotton cloth, indigo and saltpetre from fortified posts at Surat, Madras, Bombay and Calcutta, and later Chinese tea.",
 why="English merchants wanted a share of the Asian trade dominated by the Portuguese and Dutch. A joint-stock monopoly pooled capital and spread the risk of voyages that took years.",
 then="As Mughal power fragmented in the 1700s, the Company used its private army to fight French rivals and Indian states, winning the right to collect Bengal's revenues after Plassey (1757). By 1800 its army numbered about 200,000. Parliament gradually regulated it and took India over directly after the 1857 rebellion.",
 now="It is the standard example in debates over corporate power, and India's administrative system and English-speaking elite trace back to Company rule.",
 lens="When a corporation can raise armies and taxes, the line between business and government disappears. Watch firms that control critical infrastructure."),
"eu160229": dict(
 bluf="The Dutch East India Company pioneered the permanent joint-stock company with shares anyone could buy and sell, and with it the modern stock market. It used powers granted by the state to wage war and monopolize the spice trade, building a Dutch empire in Indonesia.",
 story="In 1602 the Dutch Republic merged rival trading ventures into the VOC (Dutch East India Company), with a monopoly on Dutch trade in Asia and the right to make treaties, build forts and wage war. Unlike earlier ventures, its capital was permanent, and investors could sell their shares on the Amsterdam exchange.",
 why="Dutch merchants wanted to pool money for risky voyages and beat their Portuguese and English rivals; the state, still at war with Spain, wanted one powerful company to fight the Iberians in Asia.",
 then="The VOC drove the Portuguese from much of Asia, founded Batavia, conquered the Banda Islands and monopolized nutmeg, cloves and cinnamon. It paid high dividends for decades but grew corrupt and indebted, and was dissolved in 1799; the Dutch state took over its territories.",
 now="Tradable shares, stock exchanges and the basic machinery of global capitalism descend from the VOC. Indonesia's borders largely follow the colonial state it began.",
 lens="Financial innovation often comes from war and empire: the need to fund risky, armed ventures created modern capital markets."),
"as160316": dict(
 bluf="The Tokugawa shogunate gave Japan 250 years of peace by freezing its political and social order under tight central control. Restricted foreign contact, hereditary classes and forced attendance at Edo kept the lords weak, but also built the urban, literate, commercial society that let Japan modernize so quickly after 1868.",
 why="Victory at Sekigahara left Ieyasu the strongest lord in Japan, and a century of civil war made peace and order the overriding goal.",
 then="Japan's population, cities and commerce grew; Edo became one of the largest cities in the world, and literacy spread. But when Western warships arrived in 1853 the system could not respond, and the shogunate fell in 1868.",
 now="Much of what is seen as 'traditional' Japan, from kabuki and woodblock prints to castle towns, comes from the Edo period.",
 lens="Stability by freezing a system works until the outside world changes faster than the system can."),
"oc-duyfken": dict(
 bluf="The Duyfken's landing was the first recorded European contact with Australia. The Dutch found nothing worth trading and never settled, which left the continent to its Aboriginal peoples for another 180 years until British colonization.",
 why="The VOC was searching for new trade and the rumoured 'Great South Land'.",
 then="Dutch ships charted much of Australia's west and north coasts over the next 40 years and called it New Holland, but the VOC saw no profit in it.",
 now="Aboriginal Australians had lived on the continent for at least 50,000 years before any European arrived; first-contact stories are now part of debates over how Australia tells its history.",
 lens="Explorers see a land through their sponsors' interests: the Dutch saw no trade, so they saw no value."),
"na16073": dict(
 bluf="Jamestown was England's first lasting American colony, and its survival on tobacco created the model of the plantation South: an export crop, land taken from Native peoples, and an ever-growing demand for bound labor that turned to African slavery. It also produced English America's first representative assembly.",
 story="In 1607 the Virginia Company of London, a joint-stock venture, sent about 100 men to found Jamestown in the territory of the Powhatan Confederacy. Hoping for gold, they found disease, hunger and a powerful Native state; most early settlers died, especially in the 'starving time' of 1609-10. Tobacco, planted by John Rolfe from 1612, finally made the colony pay.",
 why="England wanted its share of the Americas after Spain's success, and investors hoped for gold and a passage to Asia. The joint-stock company let merchants spread the risk.",
 then="Tobacco exports boomed and settlers spread onto Powhatan land, provoking wars in 1622 and 1644. Labor came first from English indentured servants, then increasingly from enslaved Africans. In 1619 Virginia created the House of Burgesses.",
 now="Representative government, Native dispossession and slavery, central threads of American history, all appear together in Virginia's first decades.",
 lens="Colonies take the shape of their most profitable export: a cash crop needs land and labor, and colonists will take both."),
"na16084": dict(
 bluf="Champlain's Quebec anchored New France, an empire built on the fur trade and alliances with Indigenous nations rather than mass settlement. Its legacy is a French-speaking society that has survived inside an English-speaking continent.",
 why="France wanted to control the lucrative fur trade with Indigenous partners, and Quebec's narrows commanded the St. Lawrence.",
 then="French traders and missionaries pushed deep into the interior along the rivers to the Great Lakes and the Mississippi, allied with the Wendat (Huron) and Algonquin against the Iroquois. With only about 70,000 settlers by 1760, New France could not withstand Britain, which conquered it in 1759-63.",
 now="Quebec's distinct identity, its language laws and its two independence referendums (1980 and 1995) trace back to this colony.",
 lens="Empires built on trade alliances cover huge territory lightly; empires built on settlement hold it densely. In a contest, density usually wins."),
"d-kepler": dict(
 bluf="Kepler replaced the perfect circles of ancient astronomy with ellipses, producing the first laws of planetary motion that matched observations precisely. He turned astronomy from geometry into physics and set up Newton's theory of gravity.",
 why="Copernicus's circular orbits did not match Tycho Brahe's precise measurements of Mars.",
 then="Kepler's third law followed in 1619, and Newton later explained all three with gravity.",
 now="Kepler's laws are still used to calculate the orbits of planets, moons and spacecraft.",
 lens="When the data refuse to fit, sometimes it is a hidden assumption, such as 'orbits must be circles', that has to go."),
"eu161030": dict(
 bluf="Galileo's telescope gave the first physical evidence that the Sun-centred model was right, and his work on motion made experiment and mathematics the method of physics. His clash with the Church became the defining story of science versus authority.",
 story="In 1609-10 Galileo built telescopes far better than the Dutch spyglass he had heard about and saw Jupiter's moons, the phases of Venus, sunspots and mountains on the Moon. He published his first findings in The Starry Messenger (1610), in a style any educated reader could follow.",
 why="The telescope arrived in a culture already arguing over Copernicus, and Galileo, a mathematician who believed in testing ideas by experiment, saw its potential at once.",
 then="His findings undermined the ancient Earth-centred cosmos. After the Church banned Copernican books (1616) and Galileo appeared to mock the pope in his Dialogue (1632), he was tried and confined to house arrest (1633). His later work on motion laid foundations for Newton.",
 now="Galileo is shorthand for the conflict between evidence and authority; the Catholic Church formally acknowledged its error in 1992.",
 lens="New instruments create new knowledge: ask what can now be measured that could not be before."),
"d-swally": dict(
 bluf="A small naval victory over the Portuguese earned the English East India Company permission from the Mughals to trade at Surat, its first foothold in India. It showed Mughal officials that the English could challenge Portugal at sea.",
 why="Portugal had controlled India's sea trade for a century and tried to keep rivals out.",
 then="Surat became the Company's main base in India until Bombay replaced it later in the century, and Sir Thomas Roe arrived as ambassador to Emperor Jahangir in 1615.",
 now="Britain's 335-year presence in India began not with conquest but with a trading licence from the Mughal emperor.",
 lens="Local rulers often welcome a new foreign power to balance an old one."),
"eu161831": dict(
 bluf="The Thirty Years' War began as a religious conflict inside the Holy Roman Empire and became a European struggle for power, fought mostly on German soil. It devastated Central Europe and ended in a settlement that made state sovereignty, not religious unity, the basis of the European order.",
 story="In 1618 Protestant nobles in Bohemia threw two imperial officials out of a window of Prague Castle in protest at the Catholic Habsburgs' curbs on their rights. The revolt drew in the Catholic League, Protestant princes, Denmark, Sweden and finally Catholic France, which sided with the Protestants against the Habsburgs. Armies living off the land spread famine and plague across Germany.",
 why="The 1555 Peace of Augsburg had not settled religious tensions and excluded Calvinists. The Habsburgs sought to strengthen imperial and Catholic power; Protestant princes and foreign powers such as Sweden and France wanted to limit it.",
 trig="The 'Defenestration of Prague' in May 1618, a protest against restrictions on Protestant rights in Bohemia.",
 then="Parts of Germany lost a third or more of their population. The war ended Habsburg hopes of dominating Germany, made France and Sweden great powers, and exhausted religion as a cause of war between European states.",
 now="It fixed Germany's fragmentation for two centuries. Analysts invoke it for wars that draw in many outside powers, such as Syria's.",
 lens="Religious wars turn into power struggles: when Catholic France backs Protestants, interest has overtaken faith."),
"na16195": dict(
 bluf="The sale of about 20 Africans at Point Comfort in 1619 is the earliest recorded arrival of Africans in English North America. Over the following decades Virginia turned servitude into hereditary, race-based slavery, a system that shaped the economy, Constitution and society of the future United States.",
 why="Tobacco planters needed labor, and English privateers sold captives they had seized from a Portuguese slave ship bound for Mexico.",
 then="At first Africans' status varied and some gained freedom, but from the 1660s Virginia laws made slavery lifelong, hereditary through the mother (1662) and based on race. After Bacon's Rebellion (1676), planters shifted from indentured servants to enslaved Africans.",
 now="The date is central to debates over how to tell America's story, and the legacies of slavery still shape inequality and politics in the United States.",
 lens="Hierarchies are built by law: watch how statutes turn an economic arrangement into a fixed ranking of people."),
"na16196": dict(
 bluf="The House of Burgesses was the first elected legislature in English America, the root of a tradition of colonial self-government that later made Parliament's taxes look like tyranny. It began the same year enslaved Africans arrived: freedom and slavery grew up together.",
 why="The Virginia Company offered a measure of self-government to attract settlers.",
 then="Colonists grew used to governing themselves through their own assemblies, which made later British taxes imposed without their consent feel illegitimate.",
 now="Freedom and slavery arriving in Virginia the same year captures the central contradiction of American history.",
 lens="Representative institutions often start as concessions to attract or keep people, and once granted they are hard to take back."),
"se-batavia": dict(
 bluf="Batavia became the headquarters of Dutch power in Asia and the core of a colonial state that eventually covered all of today's Indonesia. Its founding marked the shift from European trading posts to territorial control enforced by violence.",
 why="The VOC needed a secure base to control the spice trade and keep the English out.",
 then="From Batavia the VOC enforced its spice monopolies and gradually took control of Java through wars and alliances with local rulers. The city's segregated colonial society included a large Chinese community, which suffered a massacre in 1740.",
 now="Batavia is now Jakarta, Indonesia's largest city.",
 lens="Colonial capitals were built for extraction and control; their layouts often still show who was meant to live where."),
"na16207": dict(
 bluf="Plymouth was small, but the Mayflower Compact, in which settlers agreed to make and obey their own laws, became a founding text of American self-government. The colony opened New England to Puritan migration, which shaped American religious and civic culture.",
 why="The Pilgrims were Separatists who had left the Church of England and wanted to worship their own way, away from persecution.",
 then="Plymouth survived thanks to Wampanoag help, and the alliance held for decades, until King Philip's War (1675-76) destroyed Native power in southern New England.",
 now="The Compact is an early model of government by consent, and the Thanksgiving story is built on these first years.",
 lens="Founding myths simplify: the Thanksgiving story leaves out what happened to the Wampanoag fifty years later."),
"d-whitemountain": dict(
 bluf="The Habsburg victory crushed the Bohemian revolt and began a forced re-Catholicization of the Czech lands. It also turned a local rebellion into a German-wide war, because Protestant princes feared they would be next.",
 why="Bohemia's Protestant nobles had rejected the Catholic Habsburg Ferdinand as king and chosen Frederick V of the Palatinate instead.",
 then="Rebel leaders were executed or exiled and their estates handed to Catholic loyalists, many of them German-speaking, while Bohemia was ruled from Vienna. Protestant princes across Germany prepared to resist, and the war spread.",
 now="Czech nationalists remembered White Mountain as the start of 300 years of Habsburg domination, which ended only in 1918.",
 lens="Crushing a revolt can widen a war if it convinces neutral parties they are next."),
"d-banda": dict(
 bluf="To monopolize nutmeg, the VOC killed, enslaved or drove out most of the Banda Islands' people and replaced them with plantations worked by slaves. Often described as a genocide, it shows what happens when a profit-seeking company holds the powers of a state.",
 why="Nutmeg and mace grew only on Banda, and the Bandanese kept selling them to the English despite Dutch demands for exclusive rights.",
 then="The VOC held a monopoly on nutmeg for over a century.",
 now="In the 1667 peace, the Dutch kept Run, a tiny Banda island, while England kept New Amsterdam (Manhattan), a measure of how valuable nutmeg then seemed.",
 lens="Monopolies over a scarce commodity invite extreme violence to protect them."),
"na16308": dict(
 bluf="The Puritan colony brought a large, organized, family-based migration that built a literate, town-governed and intensely religious society in New England. Its ideals and its intolerance, from the 'city upon a hill' to the banishing of dissenters, shaped American civic culture.",
 why="Puritans under pressure from Charles I and Archbishop Laud wanted to build a godly community, and a royal charter let their company govern itself in America.",
 then="About 20,000 English migrated in the 'Great Migration' of the 1630s. Town meetings, schools and Harvard (1636) took root; dissenters such as Roger Williams founded Rhode Island on religious freedom. Expansion brought wars with Native peoples, from the Pequot War (1637) to King Philip's War (1675-76).",
 now="The 'city upon a hill' remains part of American political rhetoric about national mission, and New England's emphasis on education and local government persists.",
 lens="Societies founded on a mission tend to export it; the idea that America has a special role in the world starts here."),
"d-magdeburg": dict(
 bluf="The destruction of Magdeburg was the worst atrocity of the Thirty Years' War and a propaganda disaster for the emperor. It pushed key Protestant states into alliance with Sweden and turned the war against the Habsburgs.",
 why="Magdeburg defied the emperor and allied with Sweden, then waited in vain for Swedish relief.",
 then="Outrage pushed Saxony and Brandenburg into alliance with Gustavus Adolphus, who routed the imperial army at Breitenfeld four months later.",
 now="A new German verb, 'magdeburgisieren', came to mean total destruction.",
 lens="Atrocities can cost more politically than they gain militarily, by driving neutral parties to the other side."),
"as163217": dict(
 bluf="The Taj Mahal shows the Mughal Empire at its wealthiest, able to fund monumental architecture blending Persian, Islamic and Indian traditions. Its cost also reflects the heavy taxation on which Mughal splendour rested.",
 why="Shah Jahan's grief for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, and the Mughals' wish to display their wealth and piety.",
 then="Shah Jahan's building and wars strained the treasury. His son Aurangzeb seized power in 1658 after a war of succession and confined him in Agra Fort.",
 now="The Taj is India's most famous monument and a focus of arguments over how India presents its Mughal past.",
 lens="Monuments reveal a state's surplus and priorities: ask who paid for them."),
"d-lutzen": dict(
 bluf="Sweden won at Lützen but lost its king, Gustavus Adolphus, whose military reforms had made it a great power. Without him the war became a drawn-out struggle, which France joined directly to keep the Habsburgs in check.",
 why="Sweden had entered the war in 1630 to save the German Protestants and to control the Baltic.",
 then="Sweden fought on under Chancellor Axel Oxenstierna, and France declared war on Spain in 1635 and joined the German war directly.",
 now="Gustavus Adolphus's methods (mobile artillery, combined arms and a professional, well-drilled army) influenced warfare across Europe, and Sweden remained a great power in the Baltic until 1709.",
 lens="Military innovation can make a small state a great power, but often depends on one leader."),
"d-galileotrial": dict(
 bluf="Galileo's condemnation showed the Catholic Church using its authority to suppress a scientific claim. It pushed scientific publishing toward Protestant northern Europe and became the lasting symbol of conflict between science and religious authority.",
 why="A 1616 decree against Copernicanism, and a pope who felt personally betrayed by an old friend.",
 then="Science moved north: Galileo's last book, on motion, was smuggled out and printed in Leiden in 1638, and Descartes shelved a book on the Copernican universe.",
 now="In 1992 Pope John Paul II acknowledged that the Church had erred in condemning Galileo.",
 lens="When authorities punish scientists, research moves to places where it can be published freely."),
"d-descartes": dict(
 bluf="Descartes tried to rebuild knowledge on the certainty of reason alone. His method of systematic doubt and his mathematics laid groundwork for modern philosophy and science, and his decision to publish in the Dutch Republic shows where intellectual freedom then lay.",
 why="The new science needed a method, and decades of religious war made certainty precious.",
 then="Cartesian philosophy and his analytic geometry shaped science and mathematics for a century.",
 now="Every x-y graph uses 'Cartesian' coordinates, and the mind-body problem he posed still frames debates in philosophy and neuroscience.",
 lens="Start from what you can verify, and build up from there."),
"as163918": dict(
 bluf="The Tokugawa restrictions on foreign contact cut Japan off from Europe for over 200 years, apart from a Dutch post at Nagasaki. They protected the regime from Christian and foreign influence but left Japan unprepared when Western industrial powers arrived.",
 why="The shogunate saw Christianity, which had won perhaps 300,000 converts, as a threat to its authority, and wanted to control foreign trade itself.",
 trig="The Shimabara Rebellion (1637-38), a peasant revolt with many Christian participants, convinced the shogunate that Portuguese missionaries were dangerous.",
 then="Japan was never fully sealed: trade continued with China, Korea, the Ryukyu kingdom and the Dutch, and Dutch books fed a school of 'Dutch learning'. Japan developed internally until US warships forced it open in 1853.",
 now="It explains why Japan's encounter with Western power was so sudden and why its modernization after 1868 was so rapid and state-driven.",
 lens="'Closed' countries are rarely fully closed: ask which channels stay open and who controls them."),
"d-madras": dict(
 bluf="Fort St George was the East India Company's first fortified base in India, the start of a shift from trading as guests of Indian rulers to holding territory of its own. Madras became one of the Company's three centres of power in India.",
 why="The Company wanted cheap Coromandel cotton cloth and a fortified base of its own.",
 then="Madras became the first of the Company's three great 'presidencies', later joined by Bombay and Calcutta.",
 now="Chennai, as Madras is now called, is one of India's largest cities, and Fort St George still houses the Tamil Nadu state legislature.",
 lens="Fortified trading posts are the first step from commerce to colony."),
"d-malacca1641": dict(
 bluf="The Dutch capture of Malacca ended Portuguese power in Southeast Asia and confirmed the VOC as the region's dominant European force. The Dutch then let Malacca decline in favour of their own capital, Batavia.",
 why="Control of the strait meant control of trade between the Indian Ocean and China.",
 then="The VOC centred its trade on Batavia, and Malacca became a secondary port.",
 now="Malacca's Dutch buildings, such as the red Stadthuys, are part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
 lens="New dominant powers move trade to their own hubs and let rivals' cities decline."),
"eu164232": dict(
 bluf="The English Civil Wars were fought over who held ultimate authority: the king or Parliament. Parliament's victory, the execution of Charles I and the settlement of 1688 established that English monarchs rule under law and with Parliament's consent, a foundation of constitutional government.",
 story="Charles I ruled without Parliament for eleven years (1629-40), raising money by contested means and pushing church reforms that Puritans saw as Catholic. When his attempt to impose a prayer book on Scotland sparked war, he had to recall Parliament, which demanded reforms. In 1642 both sides raised armies; Parliament's New Model Army, led by Oliver Cromwell, won by 1646 and again in 1648.",
 why="Long-running disputes over taxation without Parliament's consent, over religion (fear of Catholicism versus Puritan radicalism) and over royal claims to rule by divine right, in three kingdoms (England, Scotland and Ireland) that were all in upheaval.",
 trig="Charles's attempt to arrest five members of Parliament in January 1642.",
 then="Charles I was executed in 1649, and England became a republic under Cromwell, who also brutally conquered Ireland. The monarchy returned in 1660, but the question of ultimate authority was settled in Parliament's favour in 1688.",
 now="Parliamentary sovereignty and the principle that representatives must approve taxes descend from this struggle, which also inspired the American revolutionaries.",
 lens="Fights over who controls taxation are fights over who controls the state."),
"oc-tasman": dict(
 bluf="Tasman's voyage put Tasmania and New Zealand on European maps but produced no contact beyond a violent clash with Māori. Europe's lack of interest left New Zealand to the Māori for another 127 years.",
 why="The VOC wanted to find the Great South Land and a route to South America.",
 then="No European returned to New Zealand until James Cook in 1769.",
 now="Tasmania and the Tasman Sea are named after him, and New Zealand's name comes from the Dutch province of Zeeland.",
 lens="First encounters set the tone: a violent clash discouraged contact for over a century."),
"as164419": dict(
 bluf="The Ming collapse shows how fiscal crisis, climate shock and rebellion can bring down a great state together. It let the Manchus conquer China and found the Qing, the last imperial dynasty.",
 why="The Ming faced decades of cold, drought and famine during the 'Little Ice Age', disruptions to the silver supply its tax system depended on, crushing military costs on the northern frontier, and factional paralysis at court. Unpaid soldiers and starving peasants joined rebel armies.",
 then="Li Zicheng held Beijing for only six weeks: the Ming general Wu Sangui let the Manchus through the Great Wall, and they took the capital.",
 now="The Ming collapse is a classic case study of how climate stress, debt and rebellion can topple a state.",
 lens="States rarely fall to one cause: look for climate, money, army pay and elite paralysis arriving together."),
"as164419b": dict(
 bluf="The Manchu conquest founded the Qing, China's last imperial dynasty. The Qing roughly doubled China's territory, adding Tibet, Xinjiang, Mongolia and Taiwan, and the borders of today's People's Republic largely reflect that expansion.",
 why="The Manchus, a state built north of the Wall, saw the Ming collapse as their chance, and the Ming general Wu Sangui wanted revenge on the rebels.",
 then="Qing emperors ruled through Chinese institutions while keeping a separate Manchu identity, and forced Chinese men to wear the Manchu queue. The long reigns of the Kangxi, Yongzheng and Qianlong emperors in the 1700s brought prosperity and expansion, but the dynasty struggled to respond to Western powers in the 1800s.",
 now="Beijing's claims to Tibet, Xinjiang and Taiwan rest largely on Qing-era borders, and the Qing's decline begins the 'century of humiliation' central to the Communist Party's story of itself.",
 lens="Modern borders often reflect the maximum reach of a country's last empire."),
"eu164833": dict(
 bluf="The Peace of Westphalia ended the Thirty Years' War and the Eighty Years' War and established a European order based on sovereign states that no longer fought over religion. 'Westphalian sovereignty', each state controlling its own territory, is still the formal basis of international law, even if the treaties were less revolutionary than the myth.",
 story="After years of negotiations in Münster and Osnabrück, exhausted powers signed treaties in 1648. They confirmed the German princes' right to conduct their own foreign policy, extended the 1555 religious settlement to Calvinists, recognized Dutch and Swiss independence, and gave territory to France and Sweden.",
 why="After 30 years of war, every side was exhausted and none could win outright.",
 then="The Holy Roman Empire became a loose association of hundreds of territories, and France rose as Europe's leading power. Religion largely stopped being a cause of war between European states.",
 now="The principle of non-interference in sovereign states is invoked in every debate over humanitarian intervention, from Kosovo to Syria, and China and Russia cite it often.",
 lens="International orders are born from exhaustion: big settlements come when no side can win."),
"d-munster": dict(
 bluf="Spain's recognition of the Dutch Republic ended an 80-year war and confirmed a small trading republic as a sovereign power. It marked Spain's acceptance of decline in northern Europe.",
 why="Spain was exhausted and wanted to concentrate its forces against France.",
 then="The Dutch Republic, already Europe's leading trading nation, enjoyed its golden age as a recognized power.",
 now="The Netherlands dates its independence to this treaty.",
 lens="Great powers make peace with rebels when a bigger rival demands their attention."),
"eu164934": dict(
 bluf="The public trial and execution of a reigning king in the name of the people shattered the idea that monarchs answered only to God. It set a precedent that echoed in the French Revolution and in later ideas of popular sovereignty.",
 why="Army leaders concluded they could not trust the king after he restarted the war in 1648.",
 then="England, Scotland and Ireland became a republic, ruled in practice by Cromwell as Lord Protector from 1653. After Cromwell's death the monarchy returned in 1660, and surviving regicides were executed.",
 now="It remains the most dramatic precedent that rulers can be held to account by law.",
 lens="Once a ruler has been tried by law, the idea that rulers stand above the law never fully recovers."),
}
